IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `eia-core`
IMPORT `eia-core-interpretation`
IMPORT `ei7-related-nouns`
IMPORT `ei7-related-2003-2010`
IMPORT `ei7-related-2012-tribunal`
IMPORT `ei7-related-2012-2015`
IMPORT `ei7-related-2016-2018`
IMPORT `ei7-related-2018-elections`
IMPORT `ei7-related-2020-2023`
IMPORT `ei7-seat`

-- Worked cases for ei7-seat: at least one for each of its questions, with the facts a real person in the seat would state.
-- Every expected value is written by hand from the text of the related provision the question cites (src:3599-3999); none
-- is copied from the encoding's output. Days that an amending Act leaves to "the day on which ... come into force", to a
-- royal assent or to an order are not in the deposited text, so the days given for them below are chosen to test the
-- boundary and are not the real days. Each case that answers "outside the encoding" is marked and asserts NOTHING.

§ `Which version of the Act governs my benefit period, claim or week (amendments of 2003 to 2015)`

-- Zoe's benefit period ran from November 2, 2003 to October 30, 2004. The week of Sunday, January 4, 2004 begins on the day the amendments apply from: covered.
#ASSERT `Do the 2003 amendments in sections 15 to 20 and 22 apply to this week of my benefit period` (YMD 2003 11 2) (YMD 2004 10 30) (YMD 2004 1 4)
-- The week of Sunday, December 28, 2003 began before it and her benefit period was not one that began on or after it: not covered.
#ASSERT NOT (`Do the 2003 amendments in sections 15 to 20 and 22 apply to this week of my benefit period` (YMD 2003 11 2) (YMD 2004 10 30) (YMD 2003 12 28))
-- Ann: a disclosure the Minister of Human Resources Development made under the old section 127, valid when made: still valid. Made by someone else, or not under those sections: the section says nothing.
#ASSERT `Is a disclosure or agreement made under the repealed sections 127 and 128 still valid` TRUE TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Is a disclosure or agreement made under the repealed sections 127 and 128 still valid` TRUE TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Is a disclosure or agreement made under the repealed sections 127 and 128 still valid` FALSE TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Is a disclosure or agreement made under the repealed sections 127 and 128 still valid` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Amir's benefit period was established on April 7, 2013: the amended section 14 applies. Ben's, on April 6, 2013, is not.
#ASSERT `Does section 14 as amended in 2012 apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2013 4 7)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does section 14 as amended in 2012 apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2013 4 6))
-- Carol claims for sickness for the week of Sunday, December 9, 2012; sections 15, 16 and 21 came into force on December 2, 2012 (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Do the 2012 sickness amendments to sections 18, 21 and 152.03 apply to my claim` TRUE (YMD 2012 12 9) (YMD 2012 12 2)
-- A claim for another reason is not one of the claims the section names.
#ASSERT NOT (`Do the 2012 sickness amendments to sections 18, 21 and 152.03 apply to my claim` FALSE (YMD 2012 12 9) (YMD 2012 12 2))
-- Dev's benefit period began on November 25, 2012 and ends November 23, 2013, caring for a critically ill child; the sections came into force on December 2, 2012.
#ASSERT `Do sections 23.2 and 152.061 apply to this week of my benefit period` (YMD 2012 11 25) (YMD 2013 11 23) (YMD 2012 12 2) (YMD 2012 12 2)
#ASSERT NOT (`Do sections 23.2 and 152.061 apply to this week of my benefit period` (YMD 2012 11 25) (YMD 2013 11 23) (YMD 2012 11 25) (YMD 2012 12 2))
-- Eli was confined in a jail for the week of Sunday, May 19, 2013, which ended before the 2013 Act came into force on Saturday, June 1, 2013.
#ASSERT `Are the new paragraphs 10(10)(a) and 152.11(11)(a) left out for this week of mine` (YMD 2013 5 19) TRUE (YMD 2013 6 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Are the new paragraphs 10(10)(a) and 152.11(11)(a) left out for this week of mine` (YMD 2013 5 19) FALSE (YMD 2013 6 1))
-- Fay claims for sickness for the week of Sunday, December 7, 2014; sections 247 and 248 came into force on December 7, 2014 (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Do the 2014 sickness amendments to subsections 18(2) and 152.03(1.1) apply to my claim` TRUE (YMD 2014 12 7) (YMD 2014 12 7)
#ASSERT NOT (`Do the 2014 sickness amendments to subsections 18(2) and 152.03(1.1) apply to my claim` TRUE (YMD 2014 11 30) (YMD 2014 12 7))
-- Gil's compassionate care period began on December 1, 2015 and ends on February 1, 2016: sections 12 and 23.1 as they read on January 3, 2016 apply from that day.
#ASSERT `Do sections 12 and 23.1 as they read on January 3, 2016 apply to my compassionate care benefits from that day` (YMD 2015 12 1) (YMD 2016 2 1)
-- Hana's began on January 3, 2016 itself: not "before that day".
#ASSERT NOT (`Do sections 12 and 23.1 as they read on January 3, 2016 apply to my compassionate care benefits from that day` (YMD 2016 1 3) (YMD 2016 4 3))
-- The same for a self-employed person.
#ASSERT `Do sections 152.06 and 152.14 as they read on January 3, 2016 apply to my compassionate care benefits as a self-employed person from that day` (YMD 2015 12 1) (YMD 2016 2 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Do sections 152.06 and 152.14 as they read on January 3, 2016 apply to my compassionate care benefits as a self-employed person from that day` (YMD 2015 11 1) (YMD 2016 1 2))

§ `Which version of the Act governs my benefit period, claim or week (amendments of 2016 to 2022)`

-- Ira's benefit period ran from January 1 to December 31, 2017: it began before July 9, 2017 and had not ended before it.
#ASSERT `Which provisions of the Act as they read before July 9, 2017 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2017 1 1) (YMD 2017 12 31) EQUALS LIST `ei7 s 2(1) the definition long-tenured worker`, `ei7 ss 10(13.1) to (14.1)`, `ei7 ss 12(2) to (2.8) and (6)`
-- Jo's began on July 9, 2017: no earlier text continues.
#ASSERT `Which provisions of the Act as they read before July 9, 2017 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2017 7 9) (YMD 2018 7 8) EQUALS EMPTY
-- The day fixed under subsection 231(3) is taken as August 5, 2018. Kim's benefit period began on September 2, 2018.
#ASSERT `Which provisions enacted in 2016 apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2018 9 2) (YMD 2018 8 5) EQUALS LIST `ei7 s 2(5)`, `ei7 s 7(2) the portion before paragraph (a)`, `ei7 s 7.1(1) the portion before the table, and s 7.1(3)`, `ei7 s 152.07(7)`
#ASSERT `Which provisions enacted in 2016 apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2018 8 4) (YMD 2018 8 5) EQUALS EMPTY
-- The day fixed under subsection 231(4) is taken as August 5, 2018. Lee's benefit period began on August 4, 2018.
#ASSERT `Which waiting period provisions of the Act as they read before the 2016 changes still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2018 8 4) (YMD 2018 8 5) EQUALS LIST `ei7 s 6(1) the definition waiting period`, `ei7 s 13`, `ei7 s 22(4)`, `ei7 s 152.01(1) the definition waiting period`, `ei7 s 152.15`
#ASSERT `Which waiting period provisions of the Act as they read before the 2016 changes still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2018 8 5) (YMD 2018 8 5) EQUALS EMPTY
-- Mia, a regular claimant, has a child born on November 30, 2017; sections 235 and 245 came into force on December 3, 2017 (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Does the Act as it read before the 2017 changes still govern my parental benefits` FALSE TRUE (YMD 2017 11 30) (YMD 2017 12 3)
-- Nia, a self-employed person under Part VII.1, in the same position.
#ASSERT `Does the Act as it read before the 2017 changes still govern my parental benefits` TRUE TRUE (YMD 2017 11 30) (YMD 2017 12 3)
-- Ola's child was born on December 3, 2017 itself.
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Act as it read before the 2017 changes still govern my parental benefits` FALSE TRUE (YMD 2017 12 3) (YMD 2017 12 3))
-- Pia is not claiming to care for a new-born or adopted child.
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Act as it read before the 2017 changes still govern my parental benefits` FALSE FALSE (YMD 2017 11 30) (YMD 2017 12 3))
-- Quin cares for a critically ill child; the period under 23.2(3) began on December 2, 2017; sections 237 and 247 came into force on December 3, 2017.
#ASSERT `Does the Act as it read before the 2017 changes still govern my benefits for caring for a critically ill child` TRUE (YMD 2017 12 2) (YMD 2017 12 3)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Act as it read before the 2017 changes still govern my benefits for caring for a critically ill child` TRUE (YMD 2017 12 3) (YMD 2017 12 3))
-- Rex's benefit period began on December 2, 2017 and ends November 30, 2018; he cares for a critically ill adult; sections 238 and 248 came into force on December 3, 2017.
#ASSERT `Do sections 23.3 and 152.062 apply to this week of my benefit period` (YMD 2017 12 2) (YMD 2018 11 30) (YMD 2017 12 3) (YMD 2017 12 3)
#ASSERT NOT (`Do sections 23.3 and 152.062 apply to this week of my benefit period` (YMD 2017 12 2) (YMD 2018 11 30) (YMD 2017 11 26) (YMD 2017 12 3))
-- Sue's child was born on February 28, 2019; section 307 came into force on March 1, 2019 (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Does the Act as it read before the 2018 changes still govern my parental benefits` FALSE TRUE (YMD 2019 2 28) (YMD 2019 3 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Act as it read before the 2018 changes still govern my parental benefits` FALSE TRUE (YMD 2019 3 1) (YMD 2019 3 1))
-- Tom's benefit period began on September 20, 2020; the day fixed under section 61 is taken as September 27, 2020.
#ASSERT `Does section 58 as it read before the day fixed under section 61 still govern my claim` (YMD 2020 9 20) (YMD 2020 9 27)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does section 58 as it read before the day fixed under section 61 still govern my claim` (YMD 2020 9 27) (YMD 2020 9 27))
-- Una's benefit period began on September 25, 2021: before September 26, 2021, so the fifteen provisions continue.
#ASSERT count (`Which provisions of the Act as they read before September 26, 2021 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2021 9 25)) EQUALS 15
-- Val's began on September 26, 2021: none.
#ASSERT `Which provisions of the Act as they read before September 26, 2021 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2021 9 26) EQUALS EMPTY
-- Wes's began on October 4, 2020: Part VIII.5 as it read before September 26, 2021 continues. Xia's, on September 26, 2021, does not.
#ASSERT `Does Part VIII.5 as it read before September 26, 2021 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2020 10 4)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does Part VIII.5 as it read before September 26, 2021 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2021 9 26))
-- Part VIII.1 was suspended on March 1, 2022 but not on September 25, 2022.
#ASSERT `Was Part VIII.1 suspended on this day` (YMD 2022 3 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Was Part VIII.1 suspended on this day` (YMD 2022 9 25))
-- Yan's benefit period began on October 3, 2021: the suspension continues for it. Zed's, on September 19, 2021, is outside.
#ASSERT `Does the suspension of Part VIII.1 continue for my benefit period` (YMD 2021 10 3)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the suspension of Part VIII.1 continue for my benefit period` (YMD 2021 9 19))
-- Abe's benefit period began on January 2, 2022: the provisions as they read before September 25, 2022 continue (section 335's list).
#ASSERT `Which provisions of the Act as they read before September 25, 2022 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2022 1 2) EQUALS LIST `ei7 s 6(1)`, `ei7 s 7(2)`, `ei7 s 7.1(1)`, `ei7 s 12(8)`, `ei7 s 21`, `ei7 ss 22(1), (2) and (5)`, `ei7 ss 23(1), (1.3), (4), (4.1), (5) and (6)`, `ei7 s 23.1(2)`, `ei7 s 23.2(1)`, `ei7 s 23.3(1)`, `ei7 s 28`, `ei7 s 29(a)`, `ei7 s 30`, `ei7 s 51`, `ei7 Schedule I`
#ASSERT `Which provisions of the Act as they read before September 25, 2022 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2022 9 25) EQUALS EMPTY
-- Bea's benefit period began on January 2, 2022, the day subsection 307(2) and section 323 came into force (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Do the amended paragraphs 12(3)(c) and 152.14(1)(c) apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2022 1 2) (YMD 2022 1 2)
#ASSERT NOT (`Do the amended paragraphs 12(3)(c) and 152.14(1)(c) apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2022 1 1) (YMD 2022 1 2))
-- Cal's benefit period began on September 24, 2022: Schedule V as it read before September 25, 2022 continues. Dot's, on September 25, 2022, does not.
#ASSERT `Does Schedule V as it read before September 25, 2022 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2022 9 24)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does Schedule V as it read before September 25, 2022 still govern my benefit period` (YMD 2022 9 25))

§ `Special situations: Pilot Project No. 10, weeks combined outside Canada, military parental leave and premium rates`

-- Eve was in Pilot Project No. 10; her benefit period ran from September 1, 2008 to March 1, 2009; the 2009 Act received royal assent on Thursday, March 12, 2009 (an illustrative day), so the second Sunday before is March 1.
#ASSERT `Is the maximum number of weeks for me set by Schedule I as enacted in 2009` TRUE (YMD 2008 9 1) (YMD 2009 3 1) (YMD 2009 3 12)
-- Fox's ended on February 28, 2009, before that Sunday.
#ASSERT NOT (`Is the maximum number of weeks for me set by Schedule I as enacted in 2009` TRUE (YMD 2008 9 1) (YMD 2009 2 28) (YMD 2009 3 12))
-- Gus's benefit period ran from January 3, 2010 to December 31, 2010; subsection 224(1) came into force on December 31, 2009 (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Does subsection 224(1) of the 2009 Act apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2010 1 3) (YMD 2010 12 31) (YMD 2009 12 31)
-- Hal's began on September 12, 2010, after September 11, 2010.
#ASSERT NOT (`Does subsection 224(1) of the 2009 Act apply to my benefit period` (YMD 2010 9 12) (YMD 2011 9 11) (YMD 2009 12 31))
-- Ivy: one of sections 3 to 6 applies and her benefit period has not been extended.
#ASSERT `Is subsection 55(10) of the Regulations deemed to read differently for me` TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is subsection 55(10) of the Regulations deemed to read differently for me` TRUE TRUE)
-- Jay is outside Canada with 45 weeks of benefits to combine, an applicable maximum of 40 weeks under 55(7), and a section 225 maximum of 52 weeks: the least of 45, 40 and 52.
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits may I combine while I am outside Canada` TRUE FALSE TRUE FALSE 45 40 52 EQUALS JUST 40
-- Kai is in Canada and is not referred to in subsection (8): the deemed text gives no power to combine (outside the deemed text).
#ASSERT `How many weeks of benefits may I combine while I am outside Canada` TRUE FALSE FALSE FALSE 45 40 52 EQUALS NOTHING
-- Lia's benefit period began on January 1, 2010 and ends on December 31, 2010, and her 23(2) period the same; the 2010 Act came into force on June 1, 2010 (an illustrative day).
#ASSERT `Do sections 2 and 3 of the 2010 Act apply to me` (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 12 31) (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 12 31) FALSE (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 6 1)
-- Max's 23(2) period ran from January 1 to May 31, 2010, ended before the day, and his parental leave was deferred until May 31, 2010; his benefit period runs to December 31, 2010: extended to 104 weeks from January 1, 2010, ending December 29, 2011.
#ASSERT `Until what day is my period for parental benefits extended under subsection 4(2) of the 2010 Act` (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 12 31) (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 5 31) TRUE (YMD 2010 5 31) (YMD 2010 6 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2011 12 29)
-- Nell's deferral ended on June 1, 2010, the day itself: not before it, so subsection (2) does not extend (outside the subsection).
#ASSERT `Until what day is my period for parental benefits extended under subsection 4(2) of the 2010 Act` (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 12 31) (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 5 31) TRUE (YMD 2010 6 1) (YMD 2010 6 1) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Otto's benefit period and 23(2) period both ran from January 1 to May 31, 2010: each extended to 104 weeks.
#ASSERT `Until what day is my benefit period extended under subsection 4(3) of the 2010 Act` (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 5 31) (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 5 31) TRUE (YMD 2010 5 31) (YMD 2010 6 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2011 12 29)
-- Pam's benefit period ran to June 1, 2010, the day itself: it had not ended before it, so subsection (3) does not apply.
#ASSERT `Until what day is my benefit period extended under subsection 4(3) of the 2010 Act` (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 6 1) (YMD 2010 1 1) (YMD 2010 5 31) TRUE (YMD 2010 5 31) (YMD 2010 6 1) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Premium rates: 2.2% for 2002, 2.1% for 2003, 1.95% for 2005; for 2004 the deemed sections say nothing (outside the encoding: section 66 governs).
#ASSERT `What premium rate did sections 66.1 and 66.3 set for this year` 2002 EQUALS JUST (2.2%)
#ASSERT `What premium rate did sections 66.1 and 66.3 set for this year` 2003 EQUALS JUST (2.1%)
#ASSERT `What premium rate did sections 66.1 and 66.3 set for this year` 2005 EQUALS JUST (1.95%)
#ASSERT `What premium rate did sections 66.1 and 66.3 set for this year` 2004 EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Self-employed persons: agreements and violations (2009)`

-- Quinn would enter into an agreement on September 15, 2009; royal assent was July 1, 2009 and section 16 came into force on January 1, 2010 (illustrative days).
#ASSERT `May I enter into an agreement under subsection 152.02(1) on this day` (YMD 2009 9 15) (YMD 2009 7 1) (YMD 2010 1 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`May I enter into an agreement under subsection 152.02(1) on this day` (YMD 2010 1 2) (YMD 2009 7 1) (YMD 2010 1 1))
-- Her agreement of September 15, 2009 is treated as entered into on January 1, 2010; one of March 1, 2010 is outside the section.
#ASSERT `On what day is my agreement under subsection 152.02(1) treated as entered into` (YMD 2009 9 15) (YMD 2009 7 1) (YMD 2010 1 1) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2010 1 1)
#ASSERT `On what day is my agreement under subsection 152.02(1) treated as entered into` (YMD 2010 3 1) (YMD 2009 7 1) (YMD 2010 1 1) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Rudy entered into his agreement on February 1, 2010, royal assent was December 1, 2009, and his benefit period would begin on January 1, 2011.
#ASSERT `May my benefit period under Part VII.1 begin on or after January 1, 2011 although fewer than 12 months have passed since my agreement` (YMD 2011 1 1) (YMD 2010 2 1) (YMD 2009 12 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`May my benefit period under Part VII.1 begin on or after January 1, 2011 although fewer than 12 months have passed since my agreement` (YMD 2010 12 31) (YMD 2010 2 1) (YMD 2009 12 1))
-- Sam was given a notice of violation on August 3, 2009 for a violation accumulated under section 7.1 before subsection 152.07(6) came into force.
#ASSERT `On what day is a violation I was given notice of treated as accumulated under section 152.07` TRUE (YMD 2009 8 3) EQUALS JUST (YMD 2009 8 3)
#ASSERT `On what day is a violation I was given notice of treated as accumulated under section 152.07` FALSE (YMD 2009 8 3) EQUALS NOTHING

§ `Electing a different deduction of earnings (2018, c. 12, ss. 292 to 295)`

-- Tess was eligible to elect under 77.991(2) and did not before August 12, 2018; she elects on August 12, 2018; the last notification came on July 14, 2018; she had earnings and claimed.
#ASSERT `May I elect to have my earnings deducted by the measure in section 293` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2018 8 12) (YMD 2018 7 14) TRUE TRUE
-- Uma elects on August 11, 2018: not "after August 11, 2018".
#ASSERT NOT (`May I elect to have my earnings deducted by the measure in section 293` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2018 8 11) (YMD 2018 7 14) TRUE TRUE)
-- Vic earned $100 in a week on a rate of $187: $25 above $75 is deducted. On a rate of $188 the figure is 40% of 188 = $75.20: $24.80.
#ASSERT `How much of my earnings for the week is deducted if I elect under section 293` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2018 8 12) (YMD 2018 7 14) TRUE TRUE TRUE 100 187 EQUALS JUST 25
#ASSERT `How much of my earnings for the week is deducted if I elect under section 293` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2018 8 12) (YMD 2018 7 14) TRUE TRUE TRUE 100 188 EQUALS JUST 24.8
-- Wen may elect but does not: section 19(2) governs, which section 293 does not decide (outside this section).
#ASSERT `How much of my earnings for the week is deducted if I elect under section 293` TRUE FALSE (YMD 2018 8 12) (YMD 2018 7 14) TRUE TRUE FALSE 100 187 EQUALS NOTHING
-- The 30th day after September 1, 2018 is October 1, 2018.
#ASSERT `By what day must I tell the Commission of my election under section 293` (YMD 2018 9 1) EQUALS YMD 2018 10 1
-- Xan made the election and cannot take it back.
#ASSERT `May I take back my election under section 293` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `May I take back my election under section 293` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Yuri told the Commission on October 1, 2018, the deadline itself: in time without any good cause.
#ASSERT `Is my election under section 293 treated as made in time` (YMD 2018 9 1) (YMD 2018 10 1) FALSE
-- Zoe told it on October 2, 2018 and can show good cause for the delay throughout: treated as made by the deadline. Without good cause: not.
#ASSERT `Is my election under section 293 treated as made in time` (YMD 2018 9 1) (YMD 2018 10 2) TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my election under section 293 treated as made in time` (YMD 2018 9 1) (YMD 2018 10 2) FALSE)
-- Zoe's cousin Lev told the Commission on October 1, 2026, eight years after a last notification on September 1, 2018; section 293 has no end date, so good cause for the whole delay is the only question.
#ASSERT `Is my election under section 293 treated as made in time` (YMD 2018 9 1) (YMD 2026 10 1) TRUE
-- The Commission's decision about an election cannot be reconsidered under section 112; a decision about anything else is not covered by this section.
#ASSERT `Can the Commission's decision about my election under section 293 be reconsidered under section 112` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
#ASSERT `Can the Commission's decision about my election under section 293 be reconsidered under section 112` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Alma made her election under 77.991(2) on August 11, 2018: it applies to the week of Sunday, August 12, 2018 but not to the week of Sunday, August 5, 2018.
#ASSERT `Does my earlier election apply to my earnings for this week` (YMD 2018 8 11) TRUE (YMD 2018 8 12)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does my earlier election apply to my earnings for this week` (YMD 2018 8 11) TRUE (YMD 2018 8 5))
-- Bo made a 77.98 election and the last notification came on August 7, 2016: section 295 applies. If it came on August 6, 2016, it does not (and he made no other election).
#ASSERT `Does section 295 apply to me` TRUE (YMD 2016 8 7) FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Does section 295 apply to me` TRUE (YMD 2016 8 6) FALSE FALSE)
-- Cy: section 295 applies, his benefit period was established on August 12, 2018, the week of Sunday, January 6, 2019, $100 of earnings on a rate of $187.
#ASSERT `How much of my earnings for the week is deducted if I elect under section 295` TRUE (YMD 2018 8 12) (YMD 2019 1 6) TRUE 100 187 EQUALS JUST 25
-- Di's benefit period was established on August 11, 2018: outside the section.
#ASSERT `How much of my earnings for the week is deducted if I elect under section 295` TRUE (YMD 2018 8 11) (YMD 2019 1 6) TRUE 100 187 EQUALS NOTHING
-- The 30th day after February 1, 2022 is March 3, 2022, later than February 19, 2022: February 19, 2022.
#ASSERT `By what day must I tell the Commission of my election under section 295` (YMD 2022 2 1) EQUALS YMD 2022 2 19
-- Fran's last notification was issued on March 1, 2022; February 19, 2022 had already passed (a finding, NOTES.md).
#ASSERT `By what day must I tell the Commission of my election under section 295` (YMD 2022 3 1) EQUALS YMD 2022 2 19
#ASSERT `May I take back my election under section 295` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE
-- Ed told the Commission on February 20, 2022, one day late, and can show good cause throughout.
#ASSERT `Is my election under section 295 treated as made in time` (YMD 2022 2 1) (YMD 2022 2 20) TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my election under section 295 treated as made in time` (YMD 2022 2 1) (YMD 2022 2 20) FALSE)
#ASSERT `Can the Commission's decision about my election under section 295 be reconsidered under section 112` TRUE EQUALS JUST FALSE

§ `Appeals and requests for reconsideration that were pending on April 1, 2013 (2012, c. 19, ss. 265 to 270)`

-- Bart's appeal was before a board of referees; the information relates to an application the Tribunal is seized of: it must be transferred. Information that neither relates to such an application nor is prescribed need not be.
#ASSERT `Must the body that dealt with my case transfer information about it to the Social Security Tribunal` TRUE TRUE FALSE
#ASSERT `Must the body that dealt with my case transfer information about it to the Social Security Tribunal` TRUE FALSE TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Must the body that dealt with my case transfer information about it to the Social Security Tribunal` TRUE FALSE FALSE)
-- The Tribunal itself is not one of the four bodies.
#ASSERT NOT (`Must the body that dealt with my case transfer information about it to the Social Security Tribunal` FALSE TRUE TRUE)
-- Flo filed an appeal under the old subsection 114(1) on March 31, 2013 and it had not been decided: the board of referees remains seized. Gus filed on April 1, 2013.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal filed before April 1, 2013 still before the board of referees` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 31) FALSE
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal filed before April 1, 2013 still before the board of referees` TRUE (YMD 2013 4 1) FALSE)
-- No order: October 31, 2013. An order fixing December 1, 2013 (illustrative): the day before, November 30, 2013.
#ASSERT `By what day must the board of referees decide my appeal` FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1) EQUALS YMD 2013 10 31
#ASSERT `By what day must the board of referees decide my appeal` TRUE (YMD 2013 12 1) EQUALS YMD 2013 11 30
-- Hugh's appeal was not decided by the board: the General Division becomes seized.
#ASSERT `Which body becomes seized of my appeal if the board of referees has not decided it in time` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) FALSE FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1) FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1) EQUALS JUST `ei7 the General Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
-- Ida's was decided on October 31, 2013, the last day: in time, so no other body becomes seized (outside this subsection).
#ASSERT `Which body becomes seized of my appeal if the board of referees has not decided it in time` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) FALSE TRUE (YMD 2013 10 31) FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1) EQUALS NOTHING
-- Jon's was decided on November 1, 2013: too late.
#ASSERT `Which body becomes seized of my appeal if the board of referees has not decided it in time` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) FALSE TRUE (YMD 2013 11 1) FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1) EQUALS JUST `ei7 the General Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
-- Deemed filed with the General Division on November 1, 2013, or on the day an order fixes.
#ASSERT `On what day is my appeal treated as filed with the General Division` FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1) EQUALS YMD 2013 11 1
#ASSERT `On what day is my appeal treated as filed with the General Division` TRUE (YMD 2013 9 1) EQUALS YMD 2013 9 1
-- Kay is dissatisfied with the board's decision under subsection (1): she may appeal to the Appeal Division. Leo is not dissatisfied (outside the subsection).
#ASSERT `May I appeal the board of referees' decision to the Appeal Division` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei7 the Appeal Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
#ASSERT `May I appeal the board of referees' decision to the Appeal Division` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `May I appeal a board of referees' decision that could have gone to an umpire to the Appeal Division` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei7 the Appeal Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
-- Mo's appeal to an umpire was filed on March 1, 2013 and heard on March 31, 2013: the umpire remains seized. Nan's was not yet heard.
#ASSERT `Is my appeal to an umpire, filed and heard before April 1, 2013, still before the umpire` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) TRUE (YMD 2013 3 31)
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my appeal to an umpire, filed and heard before April 1, 2013, still before the umpire` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1))
#ASSERT `By what day must the umpire decide my appeal` EQUALS YMD 2014 3 31
-- Oz's umpire had not decided by March 31, 2014; Pat's decided on March 31, 2014; Rob's on April 1, 2014.
#ASSERT `Does the Appeal Division become seized of my appeal because the umpire did not decide it in time` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) TRUE (YMD 2013 3 15) FALSE (YMD 2013 1 1)
#ASSERT NOT (`Does the Appeal Division become seized of my appeal because the umpire did not decide it in time` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) TRUE (YMD 2013 3 15) TRUE (YMD 2014 3 31))
#ASSERT `Does the Appeal Division become seized of my appeal because the umpire did not decide it in time` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 1) TRUE (YMD 2013 3 15) TRUE (YMD 2014 4 1)
#ASSERT `On what day is leave to appeal treated as granted to me by the Appeal Division after the umpire missed the deadline` TRUE EQUALS JUST (YMD 2014 4 1)
#ASSERT `On what day is leave to appeal treated as granted to me by the Appeal Division after the umpire missed the deadline` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Sal's appeal was filed on March 31, 2013 and not heard before April 1, 2013: leave is treated as granted on April 1, 2013. Tia's was heard.
#ASSERT `On what day is leave to appeal treated as granted to me for an appeal not heard before April 1, 2013` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 31) FALSE EQUALS JUST (YMD 2013 4 1)
#ASSERT `On what day is leave to appeal treated as granted to me for an appeal not heard before April 1, 2013` TRUE (YMD 2013 3 31) TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- Uri's request under the old section 120 was undecided on April 1, 2013: treated as an application made on April 1, 2013 under section 66.
#ASSERT `On what day is my request for reconsideration treated as an application under section 66` TRUE FALSE EQUALS JUST (YMD 2013 4 1)
#ASSERT `On what day is my request for reconsideration treated as an application under section 66` TRUE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
-- It relates to the General Division if a board of referees decided, to the Appeal Division if an umpire did.
#ASSERT `Whose decision does my request treated as an application relate to` TRUE FALSE TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei7 the General Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
#ASSERT `Whose decision does my request treated as an application relate to` TRUE FALSE FALSE EQUALS JUST `ei7 the Appeal Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
-- Vera made an application under section 66 on April 1, 2013 about a board's decision; Walt's, on March 31, 2013, is outside the subsection.
#ASSERT `Whose decision does my application under section 66 made after March 31, 2013 relate to` (YMD 2013 4 1) TRUE EQUALS JUST `ei7 the General Division of the Social Security Tribunal`
#ASSERT `Whose decision does my application under section 66 made after March 31, 2013 relate to` (YMD 2013 3 31) TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Do the repealed provisions of the Act still apply to my appeal` TRUE
#ASSERT NOT (`Do the repealed provisions of the Act still apply to my appeal` FALSE)

§ `Section 66 of the Department of Employment and Social Development Act (2023, c. 26, s. 676)`

-- Xena has a decision under the earlier section 66 in relation to the Employment Insurance Act.
#ASSERT `Does section 241 of the Budget Implementation Act, 2021, No. 1 stay out of my decision or application under section 66` TRUE FALSE FALSE
#ASSERT `Is my matter dealt with under section 66 as it read before Division 20 came into force` TRUE FALSE FALSE
-- Yves has an application that was ongoing immediately before section 634 came into force.
#ASSERT `Does section 241 of the Budget Implementation Act, 2021, No. 1 stay out of my decision or application under section 66` FALSE TRUE TRUE
-- Zack has an application that was not ongoing.
#ASSERT NOT (`Is my matter dealt with under section 66 as it read before Division 20 came into force` FALSE TRUE FALSE)

§ `Interim Orders of 2020`

-- A provision of the Act outside Part VIII.4 that conflicts with Part VIII.4: Part VIII.4 applies despite it. A provision of another Act: the section says nothing (outside the section).
#ASSERT `Does Part VIII.4 apply despite a conflicting provision of the Act or the Regulations` TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Does Part VIII.4 apply despite a conflicting provision of the Act or the Regulations` FALSE EQUALS NOTHING
#ASSERT `Does one of the 2020 Interim Orders apply despite a conflicting provision of the Act or the Regulations` TRUE TRUE EQUALS JUST TRUE
#ASSERT `Does one of the 2020 Interim Orders apply despite a conflicting provision of the Act or the Regulations` FALSE TRUE EQUALS NOTHING
